// Title: PEG-based transformer provides front-, middle- and back-end stages in a simple compiler
// Authors: Ian Piumarta
// Source: VPRI Technical Report TR-2010-003 (2010), Figure 6 (text to AST). Appendix B ("Code listings")
//   states that Figures 6, 7 and 8 contain the complete PEG grammars for the front, middle and back ends.
//   Printed page 10, PDF page 11 (top half of the page).
// Transcribed from the page images; text-layer differences noted below.

start   = sexpr

sexpr   = _ (atom | list)
atom    = symbol | number
list    = "(" sexpr* :l _ ")" -> :l

symbol  = ( letter (letter | digit)* ) $$
number  = digit+ $#10

letter  = [-+!\$\%&*./:<=>?@A-Z\\^_a-z|~]
digit   = [0-9]

_       = (blank | comment)*
blank   = [ \t\n\r]
comment = ";" (!eol .)*
eol     = ("\n" "\r"*) | ("\r" "\n"*)

// Transcription notes
// - Page image checked at 300 dpi for every line. Glyphs match the text layer exactly.
// - Caption (not part of listing): "Grammar describing the transformation of text (a sequence of character
//   objects) into an AST. Note that the output list structures are generated implicitly as a result of the
//   Kleene star operator."
// - Column spacing: the text layer and page image differ by a few spaces in places (the text layer puts "="
//   at column 10 in some lines and column 8 in others). The layout here follows the page image: "=" at
//   column 8 for every rule name. Interior runs of spaces are not guaranteed to match the page exactly.
// - Glyph checks: "\\" and "\$" in letter, "\t\n\r" in blank, and "$#10" are printed as shown.
// - No glyph left uncertain.
